  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Specific death rate (SDR)"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "A specific death rate is the death rate of a particular section of the population, for example an age group: $\\text{SDR}_x = \\frac{D_x}{P_x} \\times 1000$. SDRs show where mortality is high (infants and the old). The CDR is the weighted mean of the SDRs with the group populations as weights: $\\text{CDR} = \\frac{\\sum P_x\\,\\text{SDR}_x}{\\sum P_x}$."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "SDRs are high for infants and the aged"
    },
    {
     "en": "CDR = Σ(Pₓ·SDRₓ)/ΣPₓ"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A specific death rate is the death rate of one age group: deaths in the group over the population of the group, times one thousand. The crude rate is the weighted average of the specific rates.",
    "kn": "ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಮರಣ ದರ ಎಂದರೆ ಒಂದು ವಯೋಗುಂಪಿನ ಮರಣ ದರ: ಆ ಗುಂಪಿನ ಮರಣಗಳು ÷ ಆ ಗುಂಪಿನ ಜನಸಂಖ್ಯೆ × 1000. ಸ್ಥೂಲ ದರವು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ದರಗಳ ತೂಕಿತ ಸರಾಸರಿ."
   },
   "formula": "\\text{SDR}_x = \\frac{D_x}{P_x} \\times 1000"
  },
